Chapter: Interval Exhaustive
  cites Math chapter Interval

 An exhaustive containment test for the Interval kernel, in the
 ConstantRange discipline: enumerate every interval over a small
 universe, apply every transfer function, and check that the concrete
 result of the runtime's own operator on every member pair is
 contained in the kernel's answer. The universe is the sixteen
 integers -8..7, giving 136 intervals with lo <= hi and 136 x 136
 interval pairs per binary operator. The concrete operators are the
 runtime's own: +, -, *, / and int-mod are evaluated by the compiled
 test itself, so whatever the runtime does IS the spec the kernel is
 checked against. Division and modulo skip member pairs whose divisor
 is zero, where the concrete operator is undefined; the kernel must
 still answer soundly for divisor RANGES containing zero, and it does
 so by returning iv-unknown, which contains every result the scan
 checks. Union must contain every member of both inputs; intersection
 must contain every value lying in both.

 Tightness is checked separately where the transfer function is
 exact: for add, sub, and neg - no endpoint can overflow in this tiny
 universe - both endpoints of every kernel answer must be witnessed
 by a concrete evaluation at the extremal corner pair.

 Memory discipline: the inner loops are pure Integer tail recursion -
 no lists, no text, no records inside any scan. Each interval pair
 allocates exactly the two argument records and the kernel's one
 result record, built once per pair before the member scan. Counts
 ride packed in a single Integer: failures times 2^32 plus checks.

Section: Universe

  ivx-lo : Integer = 0 - 8

  ivx-hi : Integer = 7

  ivx-pack : Integer = 4294967296

Section: Operators

 Operator codes: 1 add, 2 sub, 3 mul, 4 div, 5 mod, 6 min, 7 max are
 the member-scanned binary operators; 8 union and 9 intersect check
 membership rather than an arithmetic result; 11 and 12 are the
 tightness passes for add and sub.

  ivx-concrete : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  ivx-concrete (op) (x) (y) =
   if op == 1 then x + y
   else if op == 2 then x - y
   else if op == 3 then x * y
   else if op == 4 then x / y
   else if op == 5 then int-mod x y
   else if op == 6 then (if x <= y then x else y)
   else (if x >= y then x else y)

  ivx-kernel : Integer, Interval, Interval -> Interval
  ivx-kernel (op) (a) (b) =
   if op == 1 then iv-add a b
   else if op == 2 then iv-sub a b
   else if op == 3 then iv-mul a b
   else if op == 4 then iv-div a b
   else if op == 5 then iv-mod a b
   else if op == 6 then iv-min a b
   else iv-max a b

Section: Member Scans

 The innermost loops. Each iteration adds 1 to the check count and
 2^32 to the packed accumulator on a containment failure; nothing is
 allocated. ivx-scan-id checks the value itself for membership, for
 the union and intersection passes.

  ivx-scan-y : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  ivx-scan-y (op) (x) (y) (y-max) (k-lo) (k-hi) (acc) =
   if y > y-max then acc
   else if (op == 4 | op == 5) & y == 0 then ivx-scan-y op x (y + 1) y-max k-lo k-hi acc
   else let c = ivx-concrete op x y
   in let bad = if c < k-lo | c > k-hi then 1 else 0
   in ivx-scan-y op x (y + 1) y-max k-lo k-hi (acc + 1 + bad * ivx-pack)

  ivx-scan-x : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  ivx-scan-x (op) (x) (x-max) (y-min) (y-max) (k-lo) (k-hi) (acc) =
   if x > x-max then acc
   else let acc2 = ivx-scan-y op x y-min y-max k-lo k-hi acc
   in ivx-scan-x op (x + 1) x-max y-min y-max k-lo k-hi acc2

  ivx-scan-id : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  ivx-scan-id (x) (x-max) (k-lo) (k-hi) (acc) =
   if x > x-max then acc
   else let bad = if x < k-lo | x > k-hi then 1 else 0
   in ivx-scan-id (x + 1) x-max k-lo k-hi (acc + 1 + bad * ivx-pack)

Section: Tightness

 Two checks per pair: each endpoint of the kernel answer must equal
 the concrete result at its extremal corner.

  ivx-tight2 : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  ivx-tight2 (w-lo) (w-hi) (k-lo) (k-hi) (acc) =
   let bad-lo = if w-lo == k-lo then 0 else 1
   in let bad-hi = if w-hi == k-hi then 0 else 1
   in acc + 2 + (bad-lo + bad-hi) * ivx-pack

Section: Pair Handlers

 One handler per interval pair, chosen by operator code. Codes 1..7
 build the kernel answer once and scan every member pair for
 containment. Code 8 checks that the union contains every member of
 both inputs. Code 9 checks that the intersection contains every
 value lying in both inputs; disjoint inputs give an empty scan
 range, which contributes nothing - exactly the contract. Codes 11
 and 12 check add and sub tightness at the extremal corners.

  ivx-pair-members : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  ivx-pair-members (op) (l1) (h1) (l2) (h2) (acc) =
   let k = ivx-kernel op (Interval { lo = l1, hi = h1 }) (Interval { lo = l2, hi = h2 })
   in ivx-scan-x op l1 h1 l2 h2 (k.lo) (k.hi) acc

  ivx-pair-union : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  ivx-pair-union (l1) (h1) (l2) (h2) (acc) =
   let u = iv-union (Interval { lo = l1, hi = h1 }) (Interval { lo = l2, hi = h2 })
   in ivx-scan-id l2 h2 (u.lo) (u.hi) (ivx-scan-id l1 h1 (u.lo) (u.hi) acc)

  ivx-pair-isect : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  ivx-pair-isect (l1) (h1) (l2) (h2) (acc) =
   let n = iv-intersect (Interval { lo = l1, hi = h1 }) (Interval { lo = l2, hi = h2 })
   in ivx-scan-id (iv-int-max l1 l2) (iv-int-min h1 h2) (n.lo) (n.hi) acc

  ivx-pair-tight : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  ivx-pair-tight (op) (l1) (h1) (l2) (h2) (acc) =
   let k = ivx-kernel op (Interval { lo = l1, hi = h1 }) (Interval { lo = l2, hi = h2 })
   in if op == 1 then ivx-tight2 (l1 + l2) (h1 + h2) (k.lo) (k.hi) acc
   else ivx-tight2 (l1 - h2) (h1 - l2) (k.lo) (k.hi) acc

  ivx-pair : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  ivx-pair (op) (l1) (h1) (l2) (h2) (acc) =
   if op == 8 then ivx-pair-union l1 h1 l2 h2 acc
   else if op == 9 then ivx-pair-isect l1 h1 l2 h2 acc
   else if op == 11 then ivx-pair-tight 1 l1 h1 l2 h2 acc
   else if op == 12 then ivx-pair-tight 2 l1 h1 l2 h2 acc
   else ivx-pair-members op l1 h1 l2 h2 acc

Section: Pair Enumeration

 Four nested tail-recursive loops enumerate every ordered pair of
 intervals: l1 <= h1 and l2 <= h2, all in -8..7. Every loop recurses
 on itself in tail position; the calls DOWN the nest return before
 the tail step, so stack depth is the constant four.

  ivx-loop-h2 : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  ivx-loop-h2 (op) (l1) (h1) (l2) (h2) (acc) =
   if h2 > ivx-hi then acc
   else let acc2 = ivx-pair op l1 h1 l2 h2 acc
   in ivx-loop-h2 op l1 h1 l2 (h2 + 1) acc2

  ivx-loop-l2 : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  ivx-loop-l2 (op) (l1) (h1) (l2) (acc) =
   if l2 > ivx-hi then acc
   else let acc2 = ivx-loop-h2 op l1 h1 l2 l2 acc
   in ivx-loop-l2 op l1 h1 (l2 + 1) acc2

  ivx-loop-h1 : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  ivx-loop-h1 (op) (l1) (h1) (acc) =
   if h1 > ivx-hi then acc
   else let acc2 = ivx-loop-l2 op l1 h1 ivx-lo acc
   in ivx-loop-h1 op l1 (h1 + 1) acc2

  ivx-loop-l1 : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  ivx-loop-l1 (op) (l1) (acc) =
   if l1 > ivx-hi then acc
   else let acc2 = ivx-loop-h1 op l1 l1 acc
   in ivx-loop-l1 op (l1 + 1) acc2

  ivx-run : Integer -> Integer
  ivx-run (op) = ivx-loop-l1 op ivx-lo 0

Section: Negation

 The one unary operator gets its own two-level enumeration over the
 136 intervals. Mode 1 scans every member for containment of its
 negation; mode 2 checks tightness, the endpoints witnessed by
 negating the opposite corners.

  ivx-neg-scan : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  ivx-neg-scan (x) (x-max) (k-lo) (k-hi) (acc) =
   if x > x-max then acc
   else let c = 0 - x
   in let bad = if c < k-lo | c > k-hi then 1 else 0
   in ivx-neg-scan (x + 1) x-max k-lo k-hi (acc + 1 + bad * ivx-pack)

  ivx-neg-pair : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  ivx-neg-pair (mode) (l) (h) (acc) =
   let k = iv-neg (Interval { lo = l, hi = h })
   in if mode == 1 then ivx-neg-scan l h (k.lo) (k.hi) acc
   else ivx-tight2 (0 - h) (0 - l) (k.lo) (k.hi) acc

  ivx-neg-h : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  ivx-neg-h (mode) (l) (h) (acc) =
   if h > ivx-hi then acc
   else let acc2 = ivx-neg-pair mode l h acc
   in ivx-neg-h mode l (h + 1) acc2

  ivx-neg-l : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  ivx-neg-l (mode) (l) (acc) =
   if l > ivx-hi then acc
   else let acc2 = ivx-neg-h mode l l acc
   in ivx-neg-l mode (l + 1) acc2

  ivx-run-neg : Integer -> Integer
  ivx-run-neg (mode) = ivx-neg-l mode ivx-lo 0

Section: Report

 One line per operator with the exact check and failure counts, then
 the total. The expected file pins every number: a change in any
 count is a change in what was tested, and a nonzero failure count is
 an unsound transfer function.

  ivx-line : Text, Integer -> Text
  ivx-line (name) (packed) =
   name & ": " & integer-to-text (int-mod packed ivx-pack) & " checks, " & integer-to-text (packed / ivx-pack) & " failures"

  opening : [Console] Nothing =
   let r-add = ivx-run 1
   in let r-sub = ivx-run 2
   in let r-mul = ivx-run 3
   in let r-div = ivx-run 4
   in let r-mod = ivx-run 5
   in let r-min = ivx-run 6
   in let r-max = ivx-run 7
   in let r-neg = ivx-run-neg 1
   in let r-union = ivx-run 8
   in let r-isect = ivx-run 9
   in let t-add = ivx-run 11
   in let t-sub = ivx-run 12
   in let t-neg = ivx-run-neg 2
   in let total = r-add + r-sub + r-mul + r-div + r-mod + r-min + r-max + r-neg + r-union + r-isect + t-add + t-sub + t-neg
   in act
    print-line-uni (ivx-line "add" r-add)
    print-line-uni (ivx-line "sub" r-sub)
    print-line-uni (ivx-line "mul" r-mul)
    print-line-uni (ivx-line "div" r-div)
    print-line-uni (ivx-line "mod" r-mod)
    print-line-uni (ivx-line "min" r-min)
    print-line-uni (ivx-line "max" r-max)
    print-line-uni (ivx-line "neg" r-neg)
    print-line-uni (ivx-line "union" r-union)
    print-line-uni (ivx-line "intersect" r-isect)
    print-line-uni (ivx-line "tight-add" t-add)
    print-line-uni (ivx-line "tight-sub" t-sub)
    print-line-uni (ivx-line "tight-neg" t-neg)
    print-line-uni (ivx-line "TOTAL" total)
   end

